<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/chemical-bonding">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You already know that atoms contain a nucleus and shells of electrons, and that the Periodic
    Table arranges elements by how those shells are filled. Bonding is what happens when that
    shell structure meets a simple economic fact: <strong>a full outer shell is the lowest-energy
    arrangement available to most atoms</strong>, and atoms will transfer, share, or pool
    electrons to reach it. Three strategies dominate, and each produces a material with a
    recognisably different personality:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ionic bonding</strong> — electrons are transferred outright (a metal gives, a
    non-metal takes), creating charged ions that stack into rigid crystals.</li>
    <li><strong>Covalent bonding</strong> — electrons are shared between two non-metals, either in
    small discrete molecules or in vast networks.</li>
    <li><strong>Metallic bonding</strong> — atoms surrender their outer electrons to a common
    "sea" shared by all the atoms in the chunk, which is why metals conduct, bend and shine.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One warning before we start. Saying atoms "want" full shells is a memory device, not physics.
    What every bond actually does is <strong>lower the total energy</strong>: the nuclei-electron
    arrangement after bonding is more stable than the separated atoms were. The full-shell picture
    predicts <em>which</em> compounds form remarkably well; the energy picture explains <em>why</em>.
    Keep both in view and the rest of this chapter follows.
  </p>

  <h2>Where bonds come from: electrons and the Periodic Table</h2>
  <p>
    Group numbers tell you the outer-shell situation at a glance. Group 1 elements (Li, Na, K)
    carry one lonely outer electron that is cheap to lose; Group 17 elements (F, Cl, Br) are one
    electron short and grab aggressively; Group 18 (the noble gases) already have full shells and
    bond with almost nobody. This is why the table's most violent chemistry happens where Groups 1
    and 17 meet: drop sodium into chlorine gas and the reaction runs to completion on its own,
    releasing so much heat the sodium melts and burns with a yellow flame. The product is ordinary
    table salt — utterly different in character from either silvery, water-reactive metal or
    green, poisonous gas. Bonding doesn't just connect the ingredients; it builds something new.
  </p>
  <p>
    A related distinction from the last unit matters here: <strong>elements versus compounds versus
    mixtures</strong>. Bonding is the criterion. In an element (O₂, Fe) every atom is the same kind;
    in a compound (NaCl, H₂O) different atoms are <em>chemically bonded</em> in fixed ratios, and
    only a chemical reaction — not boiling, filtering or magnetism — can separate them. A mixture
    of iron filings and sulfur looks uniform but each particle keeps its own properties; heat it,
    though, and iron sulfide forms, with new bonds and new properties. Compounds are where bonding
    lives.
  </p>

  <h2>Ionic bonding: the electron hand-off</h2>
  <p>
    Sodium (electron configuration [Ne] 3s¹) spends very little energy ejecting its single outer
    electron to become Na⁺, a stable ion with a full-shell core. Chlorine ([Ne] 3s² 3p⁵) releases
    a large amount of energy absorbing exactly that electron, becoming Cl⁻. Both ions now have
    noble-gas configurations — and they attract each other electrostatically. But they do not pair
    off into "molecules". Instead, billions of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions assemble into a giant cubic
    lattice in which every sodium ion touches six chloride ions and vice versa. The formula NaCl
    is a <strong>ratio</strong>, not a molecule: the smallest whole-number recipe for the crystal.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="table salt, derived in four steps — the classic ionic hand-off" widget="equationStep" config={naclCfg} />

  <p>
    Charge balance makes ionic formulas predictable. Magnesium (Group 2) loses two electrons to
    become Mg²⁺; oxygen (Group 16) gains two to become O²⁻ — a 1:1 match, so magnesium oxide is
    MgO. But magnesium meeting chlorine (each Cl⁻ carrying only one negative charge) needs two
    chlorides per magnesium: MgCl₂. Aluminium (Al³⁺) with oxide (O²⁻) needs charges to cancel:
    two aluminiums supply +6, three oxides supply −6, so the formula is Al₂O₃ — the main
    ingredient of ruby and sapphire and the reason aluminium metal is so hard to extract from its
    ore.
  </p>
  <p>
    The lattice explains every headline property of ionic compounds. Electrostatic attraction
    between billions of oppositely charged ions is enormous, so melting points are high (NaCl
    melts at 801 °C; MgO, with its 2+ and 2− charges, at 2,852 °C). Yet the crystals are
    <strong>brittle</strong>: slide one layer of ions by a single position and charges line up
    like-with-like, the layers repel, and the crystal cleaves. And they conduct electricity
    <em>only</em> when molten or dissolved — conduction needs mobile charge carriers, and in the
    solid the ions are locked in place. Solid salt in a circuit conducts nothing; saltwater
    conducts well (which is why the electrical safety advice is never to use hairdryers in the
    bath).
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a grain of salt is one molecule of NaCl. It is roughly
  10¹⁸ formula units stacked in a repeating cubic array — which is why salt crystals cleave into
  little cubes: the shape of the crystal <em>is</em> the shape of the lattice.</p>

  <h2>Covalent bonding: the shared pair</h2>
  <p>
    When two non-metals meet, neither can afford to donate. They compromise: overlap a pair of
    electrons between them, with the shared pair counting toward both atoms' outer shells. Two
    hydrogen atoms each contribute one electron to make H₂; two oxygens need two shared pairs to
    complete their octets, giving O=O, the double bond in O₂; nitrogen goes further, with three
    shared pairs forming the famously strong N≡N triple bond (bond energy 941 kJ/mol — nearly
    twice a typical C–H bond — which is why nitrogen gas is so unreactive and why fixing it into
    fertiliser takes the Haber process or lightning).
  </p>
  <p>
    Discrete covalent molecules behave almost opposite to ionic crystals. Within a molecule the
    bonds are strong, but <strong>between</strong> molecules only weak attractions (intermolecular
    forces) hold things together — so little energy is needed to pull molecules apart. That is why
    water boils at 100 °C and carbon dioxide sublimes at −78 °C while salts need hundreds of
    degrees: you are breaking attractions <em>between</em> molecules, never the covalent bonds
    <em>inside</em> them. Boiling water does not split water into hydrogen and oxygen. Likewise,
    molecular substances do not conduct — no ions, no free electrons (graphite is the spectacular
    exception, as we will see).
  </p>
  <p>
    Sharing is not always fair. <strong>Electronegativity</strong> measures how hard an atom pulls
    on shared electrons: fluorine is the strongest puller (3.98 on the Pauling scale), oxygen
    (3.44) and nitrogen (3.04) follow, metals trail far behind. Compare the two bonding partners:
    a difference over about 1.7 means effectively ionic; between roughly 0.4 and 1.7 the bond is
    covalent but <strong>polar</strong> (the shared pair leans toward one atom, creating a small
    negative pole and a small positive pole); under about 0.4 it is essentially pure covalent.
    Oxygen–hydrogen differences (1.24) make each O–H bond polar, and because water is bent rather
    than linear, the poles don't cancel — water molecules are overall dipoles. That single fact
    explains why water dissolves salts (its poles pry ions off the lattice and cage them), why
    water has an anomalously high boiling point for its size, and why oil (nonpolar C–H bonds)
    refuses to mix with it.
  </p>

  <h2>Giant covalent structures: molecules the size of the crystal</h2>
  <p>
    Some covalent substances never bother forming small molecules — every atom bonds covalently to
    its neighbours across the entire solid. These <strong>giant covalent (network) structures</strong>
    have properties that look ionic (hard, very high melting) for a completely different reason:
    melting them means actually breaking covalent bonds, thousands of kilojoules' worth.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Diamond</strong>: each carbon bonded to four others in a rigid tetrahedral
    network — the hardest natural material, melting near 3,550 °C under pressure, an electrical
    insulator (every electron is localised in a bond), and a superb heat conductor (the stiff
    network transmits vibrations fast — diamond heatsinks exist for laser diodes).</li>
    <li><strong>Graphite</strong>: same element, opposite character. Carbon bonded to only three
    neighbours forms flat honeycomb sheets that slide easily (pencil "lead" is sheared-off
    graphene layers) and, crucially, one spare electron per atom delocalises across each sheet —
    graphite conducts electricity along its layers and is used as electrodes and motor brushes.
    Diamond and graphite are <strong>allotropes</strong>: same atoms, different bonding, wildly
    different properties.</li>
    <li><strong>Silicon dioxide (SiO₂)</strong>, quartz and sand: every silicon bridged to four
    oxygens in a 3-D network — hard, chemically stubborn, melting around 1,710 °C, the backbone of
    glass and rock.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "covalent bonds are weak" — people conclude this from the low
  melting points of molecular substances. The comparison is inverted: the covalent bonds
  <em>inside</em> molecules are among the strongest things in chemistry (H–O: 463 kJ/mol; C–C:
  348 kJ/mol). What is weak is the attraction <em>between</em> separate molecules. Diamond versus
  wax makes the distinction vivid: both are covalent, but only one of them is molecular.</p>

  <h2>Metallic bonding: the electron sea</h2>
  <p>
    Metals take sharing to the limit: every atom releases its outer electron(s) into a common pool
    delocalised over the entire piece of metal, leaving an array of positive ions packed together
    in that mobile "sea". This model explains metals in one sweep:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Electrical conductivity</strong>: the free electrons drift when a voltage is
    applied — the current in a copper wire literally is the metallic bond in motion. Metals are
    the only elemental conductors at room temperature (graphite aside).</li>
    <li><strong>Thermal conductivity</strong>: the same fast-moving electrons carry kinetic energy
    — why metal feels cold (it drains heat from your hand quickly) and why saucepans are steel or
    aluminium.</li>
    <li><strong>Malleability and ductility</strong>: the ions are held not by fixed partnerships
    but by general attraction to the electron cloud, so layers of ions can slide past each other
    without breaking the bonding — metals bend instead of shattering. Hammer an ionic crystal and
    it splits; hammer a metal and it flattens (gold beats out to films under 100 nm thick).</li>
    <li><strong>Lustre</strong>: the free electrons respond to light's electric field and re-radiate
    it, giving metals their mirror finish.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Alloying exploits the model deliberately. Add differently sized atoms — carbon into iron
    (steel), copper into gold (22-carat jewellery), chromium and nickel into iron (stainless
    steel) — and the irregular lattice resists layer-sliding, so alloys are harder than pure
    metals but usually less conductive and less malleable. Pure 24-carat gold is soft precisely
    because its lattice is perfectly uniform and slippery.
  </p>

  <h2>One spectrum, three labels</h2>
  <p>
    Resist the temptation to sort bonds into three boxes. Bonding is a continuum of electron
    sharing: an F–F bond shares perfectly evenly; an O–H bond shares unevenly; a Na–Cl bond shares
    so unevenly that we abandon the sharing picture altogether and talk about ions. The
    electronegativity difference places any bond on that line. Even "ionic" compounds have partial
    covalent character — lithium iodide dissolves in organic solvents, which a pure ionic model
    cannot explain. The three categories are the useful landmarks, not the territory.
  </p>
  <p>
    A quick decision procedure for the exam and the lab: metal + non-metal → think ionic; two
    non-metals → think covalent (molecular unless it's diamond/graphite/quartz-type); metal +
    metal → think metallic, or an alloy. Then predict properties from the model, and check them:
    high melting and conducts when molten → ionic; low melting, no conduction → molecular covalent;
    bends, conducts as a solid → metallic.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the energy actually goes.</strong> Forming an ionic solid involves a tug-of-war:
    ionising sodium costs 496 kJ/mol, and only part is paid back by chlorine's electron affinity
    (−349 kJ/mol). On electron transfer alone, NaCl formation would be uphill by about +147
    kJ/mol. What tips the balance decisively is the <strong>lattice energy</strong> — the −787
    kJ/mol released when gaseous ions assemble into the crystal. Ionic bonding is a crystal-scale
    phenomenon; that is why isolated "NaCl molecules" barely exist and why the lattice, not the
    pair, is the right unit of thought.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Bond order and length.</strong> More shared pairs means shorter and stronger bonds:
    typical values are C–C at 154 pm and 348 kJ/mol, C=C at 134 pm and 614 kJ/mol, C≡C at 120 pm
    and 839 kJ/mol. Chemists read bond lengths off X-ray diffraction data the way engineers read
    stress gauges — a stretched bond signals strain, and an unusually short one signals extra
    bonding character.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>When the octet rule fails — usefully.</strong> Noble-gas shells are a guide, not a
    law. Boron in BF₃ is stable with only six outer electrons; sulfur in SF₆ accommodates twelve;
    and the odd-electron radicals NO and NO₂ are so reactive precisely because they cannot complete
    an octet. Meanwhile the quantum-mechanical picture replaces "overlapping shells" with molecular
    orbitals — electrons spread over the whole molecule — which explains phenomena the shell model
    cannot, such as why O₂ is paramagnetic (it has two unpaired electrons) and why benzene's six
    carbons share their electrons around the ring rather than alternating single and double bonds.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Frontier note.</strong> Chemists have now coaxed helium into a compound (Na₂He, under
    extreme pressure) and verified noble-gas fluorides like XeF₂ in ordinary conditions — the
    "inert" column isn't. Every rule in this chapter, including full-shell stability, is an
    approximation that bends when the energy ledger demands it. That flexibility — model first,
    revise when the data insists — is the habit of mind this subject rewards.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (ch. 7–8, Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure); CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics (lattice energies, melting points, bond energies); Pauling, The Nature of the Chemical Bond; Royal Society of Chemistry periodic table data.</p>
</TopicPage>
